WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.600
Our approach extends
procedural modeling methods

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.500
to generate structurally
feasible masonry buildings.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.230
Here, a procedural model
is generated from a set

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.145
of grammatical rules.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.590
Using our method,
we can automatically

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.520
find rule parameters that make
the model structurally sound.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.330
The user can declare free
parameters to be optimized.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.360
In this example, we
optimized the height.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.330
Here, we show a real-time
capture of our optimization

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.360
as it iteratively
finds parameters.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.070
The ceiling is
automatically lowered,

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:49.460
so the thin columns can resist
outward forces from the arches.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:54.650
Now, we fixed the height and
optimized the column width.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.920
And now, we optimize height and
column width simultaneously.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.310
A second usage scenario
is interactive editing

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.500
of parameters.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.700
Here, the angle of
the flying buttresses

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.060
is optimized to make
the structure stable.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.190
Next, the user
decides to increase

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:16.240
the span of the ceiling.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.010
Our system then automatically
updates the buttress angle

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.860
to maintain stability.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.960
A core motivation
for stable models

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.620
is for interaction in physically
simulated environments.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.930
In this tower of stacked
blocks, the structure

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.700
is unstable and collapses.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:41.850
This stable structure
was generated

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.010
from a 20-parameter
optimization that

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.800
adjusted the horizontal
position of each block.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.350
After a few ground shakes,
the structure collapses.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:00.400
In this stacking arrangement, a
20% factor of safety was used.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.290
After small ground
shakes, the structure

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:05.800
settles back to a
stable position.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.944
Larger perturbations caused
the tower to collapse.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.030
Here, we visualize
a stable structure

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.890
collapsing after the user
removes blocks from the model.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.320
In this simulation,
the ground shakes

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.640
and the structure collapses.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.780
And finally, a projectile
is launched at an aqueduct.

